WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.537
This module is about more than display formatting.

00:00:02.537 --> 00:00:05.427
A trailing space can create a different category.

00:00:05.427 --> 00:00:08.142
Inconsistent case can fragment groups.

00:00:08.142 --> 00:00:12.117
A date stored as text cannot safely participate in date arithmetic.

00:00:12.117 --> 00:00:14.802
Time values also have different meanings.

00:00:14.802 --> 00:00:21.347
A calendar date is different from a local clock timestamp, and both are different from an instant stored with

00:00:21.347 --> 00:00:25.462
time-zone meaning. The goal is to prepare these values without accidentally changing what they mean.

00:00:25.462 --> 00:00:28.727
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:28.727 --> 00:00:32.662
a hospital calculating length of stay and follow-up dates.

00:00:32.662 --> 00:00:37.007
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:37.007 --> 00:00:40.127
analyst can understand our work.

00:00:40.127 --> 00:00:45.762
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to defend later:

00:00:45.762 --> 00:00:49.737
what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:00:49.737 --> 00:00:52.131
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
